---
import DocsLayout from '@/layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import { Callout, DocCards, StepHeading } from '@/molecules/DocComponents/DocComponents';
---

<DocsLayout breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Docs', href: '/docs' }, { label: 'Get Started', href: '/docs/getting-started' }, { label: 'Installation' }]}>
  <h1>Installation</h1>
  <p>This is the deployment sequence for the Manager-mediated architecture. Use the release-specific configuration and operator guide in the <a href="https://github.com/The-AgenticFlow/OpenFlows/tree/main/docs/implementation/centralized-deployment" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">OpenFlows repository</a> for exact commands and supported infrastructure settings.</p>

  <StepHeading step="1" id="prerequisites">Prepare services and domains</StepHeading>
  <ul>
    <li>PostgreSQL for Manager state and a database for Coder.</li>
    <li>Redis reachable from Manager and Coder workspaces; Manager needs administrative access to create tenant ACL identities.</li>
    <li>Coder with a provisioner and a network path to workspace agents.</li>
    <li>A public HTTPS origin for Manager so GitHub OAuth, App installation callbacks, and webhooks can reach it.</li>
    <li>A GitHub OAuth App for sign-in and a GitHub App for repository installation and brokered runtime access.</li>
    <li>A secret provider, encryption master key, Coder operator credential, and template release source.</li>
  </ul>

  <StepHeading step="2" id="manager">Configure and validate Manager</StepHeading>
  <p>Provide the Manager's typed deployment configuration and secret references. Run <code>openflows-manager setup</code> to bootstrap supported Coder integration, then <code>openflows-manager doctor</code> to validate secrets, PostgreSQL, Redis, Coder identity, public origin, template source, and the provisioner. Resolve failed checks before serving traffic.</p>

  <StepHeading step="3" id="serve">Start the control plane</StepHeading>
  <p>Run <code>openflows-manager serve</code> behind HTTPS. The Manager serves the product API and web UI, receives GitHub callbacks and Coder lifecycle hooks, and runs durable background workers. Coder executes the workspaces; Nexus is provisioned inside a tenant.</p>

  <StepHeading step="4" id="organization">Create the first tenant</StepHeading>
  <ol>
    <li>Sign into Manager with GitHub.</li>
    <li>Create an OpenFlows organization and wait for its provisioning operation to succeed.</li>
    <li>Install the GitHub App for the intended account and repository. Verify that the connection appears in OpenFlows.</li>
    <li>Create a tenant for an accessible repository. Manager creates the creator-owned Coder identity, tenant Redis access, and Nexus workspace.</li>
    <li>Confirm that Nexus registers, heartbeats, and reports a running workspace before opening a test issue.</li>
  </ol>

  <Callout type="warning" title="Template releases are immutable">After changing a template, publish a new release name, promote it to the organization, and update or rebuild affected workspaces. Restarting Manager alone does not replace an existing Coder template version.</Callout>
  <DocCards cards={[
    { icon: '🔑', title: 'Token Guide', desc: 'Understand sign-in, runtime sessions, Redis, and GitHub App tokens.', href: '/docs/getting-started/token-guide' },
    { icon: '🚀', title: 'First Issue', desc: 'Run the first ticket through the agent team.', href: '/docs/getting-started/first-issue' },
    { icon: '🏗️', title: 'Deployment', desc: 'Review service connections and runtime topology.', href: '/docs/architecture/deployment' },
  ]} />
</DocsLayout>
